! tutorial: fix_a_step
! title: Fixing a step that failed
! about: A step goes red when it cannot be done. Find out why, fix it, and make the part bigger without breaking it. About 3 minutes.
! order: 3
! start: H = 10
! start: BLOCK, 0, 0, 0, 30, 20, H
! start: NAME, LAST, tab
! start: SEL, S, EDGE, AT, 15, 0, H
! start: FILLET, 5
! start: SEL, NONE
! start: HIST, EDIT, 1, "H = 4"

## A step failed
The **History** panel shows the steps of this small part. Its thickness `H`
was just changed from 10 to 4, and now the FILLET step is red: a rounding of
5 does not fit on a plate only 4 thick.

Click the red step. The bar under the 3D view says what went wrong, and the
edge at fault is shown in red. Click **Why?** for the whole story, then
**Next**.
hint: WHY

## Fix it
Make the fillet smaller. Click **Find fixes** in the bar and choose one, or
click the FILLET step and change its radius to **2** in the Properties
panel.
hint: HIST, EDIT, 5, "FILLET, 2"
check: FAILED(), EQ, 0 ! no failed steps
check: NFACES(tab, "cylinder"), GE, 1 ! the edge is rounded

## Make it thicker
Set `H` back to **10** in the **Parameters** panel (or click the first
step and change it). The block and the fillet are made again on the thicker
plate, and both still work.
hint: HIST, EDIT, 1, "H = 10"
check: BBOX(tab).dz, EQ, 10, 0.01 ! the plate is 10 thick
check: FAILED(), EQ, 0 ! no failed steps

## Done
A red step never loses your work: fix the number, and every step after it
runs again. UNDO (Ctrl+Z) takes back any change.
